When you increase the TensorFlow Lite Micro arena to 1.5 MB in PSRAM on an ESP32-S3, you push the memory subsystem into a highly volatile threshold where the camera buffer, the UI heap, and the esp-nn accelerated tensors directly conflict.The freeze occurring exactly at Invoke() during your esp-nn retest points to three interconnected memory issues:1. esp-nn Cache Eviction and Latency SpikesStandard TensorFlow Lite Micro code can sit safely in PSRAM because it is accessed sequentially. However, esp-nn optimizes operations by pulling weights and activations into the internal L2 cache block-by-block using SIMD instructions.The Overlap: A massive 1.5 MB arena footprint means that every time Invoke() executes, it completely flushes out the camera's DMA frame buffer and the UI's display cache from the ESP32-S3's cache lines.The Result: The CPU stalls indefinitely waiting for PSRAM to fetch missing instructions, triggering a hard freeze or watchdog lockup exactly as inference begins.2. Camera Frame Buffer and PSRAM Bandwidth StarvationThe screen shows an active display layout (VelaPaw - Recognize) with a processed image frame. If your camera capture pipeline (esp32-camera) is configured to capture frames directly into PSRAM (to save internal SRAM), it shares the exact same SPI bus as your 1.5 MB tensor arena.When Invoke() executes with esp-nn optimizations, it floods the PSRAM bus with read/write requests.This starves the active camera or display DMA channel. If the camera driver cannot write its next frame to PSRAM within its hardware clock window, it creates an unrecoverable driver block or a silent system freeze.3. Scratch Buffer vs. Arena MiscalculationWhen you switch to esp-nn optimized kernels, the operators often demand a completely different scratch buffer size than standard reference kernels.If you merely increased the total arena size but your micro-allocator partitions the internal persistent vs. non-persistent spaces poorly, esp-nn might be overlapping its temporary execution variables right over the embed output or camera pointer region.Immediate Isolation StepsTo determine if this is a strict configuration overlap or a true per-inference memory leak, try these adjustments:Test a Hardcoded Memory Location: Instead of letting the allocator dynamically grab a 1.5 MB block from the general PSRAM heap, explicitly allocate your tensor arena as a static global array aligned to a 16-byte boundary to force it into a clean memory neighborhood:cpp__attribute__((aligned(16))) static uint8_t tensor_arena[1500 * 1024];
Use code with caution.Profile the Heap Pre- and Post-Invoke: Print out your available heap spaces right before Invoke() to confirm nothing is leaking or fragmenting:cppprintf("Free internal heap: %d KB\n", heap_caps_get_free_size(MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL) / 1024);
printf("Free PSRAM heap: %d KB\n", heap_caps_get_free_size(MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM) / 1024);
Use code with caution.Check sdkconfig Memory Settings: Ensure CONFIG_SPIRAM_MALLOC_RESERVE_INTERNAL is set to at least 32768 (32 KB). This prevents large PSRAM allocations from accidentally starving the critical internal stack memory required by system tasks during the heavy esp-nn calculations.

